---
/**
 * The pre-paint half of the theme runtime, and one of only two scripts the site
 * ships — the other is ThemeToggle.astro, the control a visitor actually clicks.
 * Nothing else on the site runs client JavaScript.
 *
 * Render it inside <head>. Anywhere in <head> is correct, because a render-blocking
 * stylesheet <link> holds off the first paint too; what matters is that it never
 * moves to <body>, where the browser can paint a head-styled document before
 * reaching it. Early in <head> is nonetheless the faster spot: a classic script
 * placed after a still-downloading stylesheet is blocked on that stylesheet.
 *
 * It has to be BLOCKING and INLINE. Blocking, because the attributes it writes decide
 * which palette tokens.css resolves; if the browser paints before it runs, a visitor
 * who chose light gets a black flash and a visitor who chose dark gets a white one.
 * Inline, because an external file — even a cached one — is a separate fetch that can
 * lose that race, and a `type="module"` script (which is what Astro emits without
 * `is:inline`) is deferred by definition and would always lose it.
 *
 * Storage contract, shared with whatever renders the theme toggle:
 *   `thor-theme`  — 'light' | 'dark'. ABSENT means "follow the system", which is how
 *                   the app's three-state ThemeMode is represented here.
 *   `thor-amoled` — 'true' | 'false'. Only meaningful while the resolved theme is
 *                   dark, mirroring the app, where AMOLED is nested under dark.
 *
 * A toggle that switches back to "follow the system" must remove `thor-theme` rather
 * than write a third value, and is also what owns listening for OS changes while in
 * that state — this script runs once, before paint, and never again.
 *
 * What a toggle must NOT do is remove the `data-theme` *attribute* to mean "system".
 * It looks equivalent, because `:root` plus the `prefers-color-scheme` media query
 * resolve the same colours with the attribute absent — but tokens.css keys AMOLED on
 * `[data-theme='dark'][data-amoled='true']`, and that selector cannot match without a
 * concrete `data-theme`. Dropping the attribute therefore switches AMOLED off again
 * immediately after this script switched it on, which reads as a #000000 → #0e0e0e
 * flash. "Follow the system" is `thor-theme` absent from storage and `data-theme`
 * still resolved to 'light' or 'dark' in the DOM, exactly as it is written below.
 */
---

<script is:inline>
  ;(function () {
    var root = document.documentElement
    var theme, amoled
    try {
      theme = localStorage.getItem('thor-theme')
      amoled = localStorage.getItem('thor-amoled')
    } catch (e) {
      // Private mode and blocked storage both throw on read. Fall through to the OS.
    }
    if (theme !== 'light' && theme !== 'dark') {
      // Dark-first: `prefers-color-scheme: no-preference` was dropped from Media
      // Queries Level 5, so a machine with no preference reports `light`. Asking for
      // light explicitly, and defaulting everything else to dark, is the closest a
      // browser can get to "dark unless you asked otherwise".
      theme = window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: light)').matches ? 'light' : 'dark'
    }
    root.dataset.theme = theme
    // AMOLED defaults FALSE. ThorTheme declares `amoledMode: Boolean = true`, but both
    // call sites pass `prefs.useAmoled`, which is false — so false is what the app
    // actually renders, and #0e0e0e, not black, is the dark page a user sees.
    root.dataset.amoled = theme === 'dark' && amoled === 'true' ? 'true' : 'false'
  })()
</script>
